---
title: "Project ArHa: Arenaviruses & Hantaviruses"
subtitle: "A global synthesis of rodent-borne viral pathogens"
title-block-banner: true
---

With Dr. [Stephanie Seifert](https://vetmed.wsu.edu/profile/seifert-stephanie/) we are leading a project synthesising published sampling records of small-mammals for arenaviruses and hantaviruses. This work is being supported by the Fellows-in-Residence programme of the [Verena consortium](https://www.viralemergence.org/). We are collaborating with Dr. [David Redding](https://www.zsl.org/about-zsl/our-people/dr-david-redding) and his group at the Natural History Museum in London.

A draft of the web-based application that will accompany the project has been developed and is available [here](https://diddrog11.shinyapps.io/arenavirus_hantavirus_app/).

## Where hosts have been sampled

```{=html}
<div class="arha-globe">
  <div class="globe-stage">
    <canvas id="arha-globe-canvas" tabindex="0" role="img"
            aria-label="Globe of Project ArHa host sampling records, binned into hexagons. Loading."></canvas>
    <div class="globe-tooltip" hidden></div>
  </div>
  <div class="globe-side">
    <p class="globe-legend-title">Host sampling records per hexagon</p>
    <ul class="globe-legend" id="arha-globe-legend"></ul>
    <p id="arha-globe-caption">Loading the sampling record.</p>
    <p>Drag to rotate, or focus the globe and use the arrow keys. The records themselves are in the <a href="https://diddrog11.shinyapps.io/ArHa_Database_Explorer/">ArHa database explorer</a>.</p>
    <details class="globe-table">
      <summary>Show as a table</summary>
      <table class="table table-sm">
        <caption id="arha-globe-table-caption">Host sampling records by country.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Country</th><th scope="col">Records</th><th scope="col">Locations</th><th scope="col">Individuals</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="arha-globe-table-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<script src="/assets/js/d3.v7.9.0.min.js"></script>
<script>
(() => {
  // Data are built by scripts/build_globe_data.R into assets/data/. Nothing
  // here fetches from a third party; d3 is self-hosted under assets/js/.
  const canvas = document.getElementById("arha-globe-canvas");
  if (!canvas || !window.d3) return;
  const stage = canvas.parentElement;
  const tip = stage.querySelector(".globe-tooltip");
  const legend = document.getElementById("arha-globe-legend");
  const caption = document.getElementById("arha-globe-caption");

  // Records per cell are heavily skewed (median ~15, max ~16,000), so the
  // ramp uses round, roughly logarithmic breaks rather than a linear scale.
  const breaks = [5, 20, 100, 500];
  const labels = ["1 to 4", "5 to 19", "20 to 99", "100 to 499", "500 or more"];
  const classOf = n => { const i = breaks.findIndex(b => n < b); return i === -1 ? breaks.length : i; };
  const cellArea = { 1: "609,000", 2: "86,700", 3: "12,400", 4: "1,770" };
  const fmt = d3.format(",");
  const plural = (n, one, many) => `${fmt(n)} ${n === 1 ? one : many}`;

  // Colours come from the theme's custom properties, so light and dark
  // follow the site toggle without duplicating values in script.
  const token = name => getComputedStyle(document.body).getPropertyValue(name).trim();
  let colours = {};
  const readColours = () => {
    colours = {
      sea: token("--globe-sea"), land: token("--globe-land"), gap: token("--ds-ground"),
      line: token("--ds-hero-line"), ink: token("--ds-ink"),
      seq: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map(i => token(`--seq-${i}`))
    };
  };

  readColours();

  // Opens centred on Africa, where the gaps in coverage are widest.
  const projection = d3.geoOrthographic().rotate([-20, -5]).clipAngle(90).precision(0.3);
  const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
  const path = d3.geoPath(projection, ctx);
  const sphere = { type: "Sphere" };
  const graticule = d3.geoGraticule10();
  let land = null, cells = [], hovered = null, size = 0;

  // d3-geo reads a ring enclosing more than a hemisphere as the complement
  // of the shape, so any polygon that comes out that way is reversed.
  const reverseRings = g => {
    if (g.type === "Polygon") g.coordinates = g.coordinates.map(r => r.slice().reverse());
    if (g.type === "MultiPolygon") g.coordinates = g.coordinates.map(p => p.map(r => r.slice().reverse()));
  };
  const fixWinding = f => { if (d3.geoArea(f) > 2 * Math.PI) reverseRings(f.geometry); return f; };

  const draw = () => {
    if (!size) return;
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, size, size);
    ctx.beginPath(); path(sphere); ctx.fillStyle = colours.sea; ctx.fill();
    ctx.beginPath(); path(graticule); ctx.strokeStyle = colours.line; ctx.lineWidth = 0.5; ctx.stroke();
    if (land) { ctx.beginPath(); path(land); ctx.fillStyle = colours.land; ctx.fill(); }
    colours.seq.forEach((fill, k) => {
      ctx.beginPath();
      for (const c of cells) if (c.k === k) path(c);
      ctx.fillStyle = fill; ctx.fill();
    });
    ctx.beginPath(); for (const c of cells) path(c);
    ctx.strokeStyle = colours.gap; ctx.lineWidth = 0.6; ctx.stroke();
    if (hovered) { ctx.beginPath(); path(hovered); ctx.strokeStyle = colours.ink; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.stroke(); }
    ctx.beginPath(); path(sphere); ctx.strokeStyle = colours.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.stroke();
  };

  const resize = () => {
    size = stage.clientWidth;
    const dpr = window.devicePixelRatio || 1;
    canvas.width = Math.round(size * dpr);
    canvas.height = Math.round(size * dpr);
    ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
    projection.translate([size / 2, size / 2]).scale(size / 2 - 2);
    draw();
  };

  const hideTip = () => { tip.hidden = true; if (hovered) { hovered = null; draw(); } };
  const showTip = (c, x, y) => {
    const { r, i, s } = c.properties;
    tip.replaceChildren();
    const head = document.createElement("strong");
    head.textContent = plural(r, "record", "records");
    tip.append(head, document.createTextNode(plural(i, "individual", "individuals")),
               document.createElement("br"), document.createTextNode(plural(s, "species", "species")));
    tip.style.left = `${x}px`; tip.style.top = `${y}px`; tip.hidden = false;
  };

  const rotateBy = (dLon, dLat) => {
    const [lon, lat] = projection.rotate();
    projection.rotate([lon + dLon, Math.max(-90, Math.min(90, lat + dLat))]);
    draw();
  };

  d3.select(canvas).call(d3.drag().on("start", hideTip).on("drag", e => {
    const k = 75 / projection.scale();
    rotateBy(e.dx * k, -e.dy * k);
  }));

  canvas.addEventListener("pointermove", e => {
    if (!cells.length) return;
    const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
    const x = e.clientX - rect.left, y = e.clientY - rect.top;
    const dx = x - size / 2, dy = y - size / 2;
    if (dx * dx + dy * dy > (size / 2) ** 2) return hideTip();
    const ll = projection.invert([x, y]);
    const hit = cells.find(c => d3.geoContains(c, ll)) || null;
    if (!hit) return hideTip();
    if (hit !== hovered) { hovered = hit; draw(); }
    showTip(hit, x, y);
  });
  canvas.addEventListener("pointerleave", hideTip);

  canvas.addEventListener("keydown", e => {
    const step = { ArrowLeft: [10, 0], ArrowRight: [-10, 0], ArrowUp: [0, -10], ArrowDown: [0, 10] }[e.key];
    if (!step) return;
    e.preventDefault(); hideTip(); rotateBy(...step);
  });

  // Quarto switches light and dark by swapping a class on <body>.
  new MutationObserver(() => requestAnimationFrame(() => { readColours(); draw(); }))
    .observe(document.body, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ["class"] });
  new ResizeObserver(resize).observe(stage);

  labels.forEach((text, k) => {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    const sw = document.createElement("span");
    sw.className = "swatch";
    sw.style.background = `var(--seq-${k + 1})`;
    li.append(sw, document.createTextNode(text));
    legend.append(li);
  });

  Promise.all([d3.json("/assets/data/land_110m.json"), d3.json("/assets/data/arha_host_hex.json")])
    .then(([l, h]) => {
      l.features.forEach(fixWinding);
      land = l;
      cells = h.cells.map(([r, i, s, b]) => {
        const ring = [];
        for (let j = 0; j < b.length; j += 2) ring.push([b[j], b[j + 1]]);
        const f = fixWinding({ type: "Feature", properties: { r, i, s }, geometry: { type: "Polygon", coordinates: [ring] } });
        f.k = classOf(r);
        return f;
      });
      const m = h.meta;
      const area = cellArea[m.h3_resolution] ? ` of about ${cellArea[m.h3_resolution]} km² each` : "";
      caption.textContent = `${fmt(m.n_records)} host sampling records from ${fmt(m.n_locations)} locations in ${m.n_countries} countries, grouped into ${fmt(m.n_cells)} hexagons${area}. Empty and pale regions show where small mammals have rarely been sampled for these viruses, not where the viruses are absent. Project ArHa data, ${m.source_modified}.`;
      canvas.setAttribute("aria-label", `Globe of Project ArHa host sampling records. ${caption.textContent}`);

      readColours();
      resize();

      // Table view: the same records by country, so no value is reachable
      // only by hovering. Built after the globe has drawn, and guarded, so a
      // problem here can never stop the globe. Quarto rewrites HTML tables and
      // drops ids on <caption>, so the caption is found through the table.
      try {
        const tbody = document.getElementById("arha-globe-table-body");
        for (const [country, records, locations, individuals] of (h.countries || [])) {
          const tr = document.createElement("tr");
          for (const v of [country, fmt(records), fmt(locations), fmt(individuals)]) {
            const td = document.createElement("td");
            td.textContent = v;
            tr.append(td);
          }
          tbody.append(tr);
        }
        const tableCaption = tbody.closest("table")?.querySelector("caption");
        if (tableCaption && m.n_records_no_country) {
          tableCaption.textContent = `Host sampling records by country. ${fmt(m.n_records_no_country)} records with coordinates but no country code are not shown.`;
        }
      } catch (e) {
        console.warn("Globe table view could not be built", e);
      }
    })
    .catch(() => { caption.textContent = "The globe could not load its data."; });
})();
</script>
```



## Papers

::: {.pub-entry .pub-entry-plain #pub-10_64898_2026_06_10_731004}
::: {.pub-body}
**Molecular and ecological determinants of effective reassortment in orthohantaviruses**  
Rivero R, **Simons D**, Damodaran L, Karegi I, Gurev S, Becker DJ, et al.  
[Preprint, 2026](https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.10.731004) · [PDF (preprint)](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.06.10.731004v1.full.pdf) 

<details class="pub-more" open>
<summary>Summary</summary>

::: {.pub-more-body}

Segmented RNA viruses can exchange whole genome segments when two lineages infect the same host, but most reassortants never establish. Why some persist and others do not is an open problem in viral evolution, and orthohantaviruses offer a tractable case because host associations are well described.

Reassortant histories were reconstructed across 553 genomes from seven orthohantavirus species sampled between 1983 and 2024, using phylogenetic reconciliation and molecular dating, then modelled with Bayesian hierarchical models. Retained reassortment varied by species, absent in Andes virus and frequent in Dobrava-Belgrade, Sin Nombre, Seoul, Puumala and Tula viruses, so it is not a genus-wide constant. Local host overlap was the strongest ecological correlate, while cross-segment linkage and terminal RNA structure acted as a molecular filter.

Establishment was most probable where ecological opportunity coincided with molecular permissiveness, their interaction being the strongest signal in the establishment models (posterior probability 0.97). Reassortment therefore appears to be sequentially filtered: lineages must first meet in a host, then exchange compatible segments, then land in a lineage background that permits establishment. 

:::

</details>

:::
:::

::: {.pub-entry .pub-entry-plain #pub-10_64898_2026_06_03_729761}
::: {.pub-body}
**Viral reservoir status in small mammals emerges as a predictable life-history trait after correcting for surveillance bias**  
**Simons D**, Rivero R, Rickard G, Martinez-Checa A, Gordon H, Redding DW, Seifert SN  
[Preprint, 2026](https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.03.729761) 

<details class="pub-more" open>
<summary>Summary</summary>

::: {.pub-more-body}
![Macroecological associations with reservoir status, holding historical sampling volume constant.](/arha/figures/fig2_traits.png){.pub-fig fig-alt="Effect estimates showing that a fast life history and synanthropy each independently raise the probability of reservoir status, holding sampling effort constant."}


Reservoir competence for arenaviruses and hantaviruses appears to track synanthropy, but surveillance has preferentially targeted synanthropic species in high-income regions. Whether that association is biological or an artefact of where sampling has occurred could not be separated without a record of effort as well as of detection.

Using the ArHa database of global sampling records, taxonomic and geographic bias were quantified first. Surveillance is skewed towards the Palearctic and towards widespread, large-bodied species; 46% of rodent and shrew genera remain entirely unsampled after fifty years, and sampling intensity correlates with accessibility and night-light intensity rather than with host biodiversity. Statistically correcting for historical sampling volume, reservoir status is then estimated as a predictable trait: a fast pace of life, meaning early maturity, large litters and small body mass, independently predicts it, and fully synanthropic species show roughly 1.8-fold higher odds of reservoir competence.

Mapping intrinsic biological hazard rather than reactive spillover risk suggests land-use change acts as an ecological filter, with species-poor disturbed landscapes sometimes harbouring communities of higher intrinsic reservoir potential than intact tropical forest. 

:::

</details>

:::
:::

::: {.pub-entry .pub-entry-plain #pub-10_12688_wellcomeopenres_24037_2}
::: {.pub-body}
**Protocol to produce a systematic Arenavirus and Hantavirus host-pathogen database: Project ArHa.**  
**Simons D**, Rivero R, Guiote AMC, Gordon HLM, Milne GC, Rickard G, Redding DW, Seifert SN  
[Preprint, 2025](https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.01.17.633514) · [*Wellcome Open Research*, 2025](https://doi.org/10.12688/wellcomeopenres.24037.2) · [PDF](https://wellcomeopenresearch.org/articles/10-227/v2/pdf) 

<details class="pub-more" open>
<summary>Summary</summary>

::: {.pub-more-body}
![Sampling locations by virus family. The holes in the coverage are the finding.](/arha/figures/sampling_locations_by_virus.png){.pub-fig fig-alt="World map of small-mammal sampling locations for arenaviruses and hantaviruses, points coloured by virus family, with dense coverage in Europe and North America and sparse coverage across Africa and Asia."}


Arenaviruses and hantaviruses are hosted primarily by rodents and shrews and cause substantial human morbidity, yet their global distribution is known only through a literature scattered across decades, languages and disciplines. Without a synthesis, sampling effort cannot be separated from pathogen occurrence.

This protocol specifies how Project ArHa assembles that synthesis: the search strategy across bibliographic databases, screening and inclusion criteria, extraction fields, and a relational structure of five tables covering citations, study design, host occurrence, pathogen assay and genetic sequence. Critically, non-detections are retained, so absence of virus in a sampled host is recorded rather than lost.

The resulting database is spatially and temporally explicit, which makes it usable for quantifying surveillance bias as well as for modelling host-pathogen association. An accompanying Shiny application opens the data to users who do not code. The protocol was published before extraction completed, fixing the method in advance. 

:::

</details>

